  "summary": "Today's health-tech news highlights several cross-cutting challenges facing the industry. The FDA rejected Capricor's Duchenne muscular dystrophy cell therapy while researchers made progress on a potential blood test for CTE and early-stage results emerged for Alterome's KRAS cancer drug, underscoring ongoing questions about drug development timelines and efficacy standards. On the policy front, fifteen consumer advocacy groups are pushing Congress to reform drug patent laws to counter pharmaceutical companies' pricing strategies, and UnitedHealth is suing to reverse downgraded Medicare Advantage quality scores, reflecting broader tensions over healthcare costs and accountability. Interoperability and data standards continue to emerge as critical infrastructure challenges, with discussions around bridging standards gaps and managing EHR sprawl costs, while artificial intelligence is gaining attention as both a tool for drug discovery and care navigation and a concern requiring data security measures. Medicare is piloting \"food is medicine\" and lifestyle intervention programs to address chronic disease prevention, and patients seeking brain implants are organizing to gain more agency in trial design, suggesting a growing push for patient-centered approaches across the system.",
